    "title": "Arkansas basketball coach John Calipari facing former player Cam'Ron Fletcher in NCAA Tournament vs. High Point",
    "text": "PORTLAND, Ore. — Rob Martin has long looked up to Cam’Ron Fletcher.Both are from the St. Louis area, and Fletcher always got the better of Martin when they competed against each other at Vashon High School and Christian Brothers College Prep, respectively. Their relationship extends 15 years, but now, at High Point, they are on the same team for the first time.It is an experience Martin has cherished. He remembers seeing his friend get recruited, namely by coach John Calipari at Kentucky.“He put in so much work,” Martin said. “Being able to get all of those looks and play at a high level, he got the exposure.”A 4-star high school prospect in the class of 2020, Fletcher played for Calipari and the Wildcats for the 2020-21 season. He appeared in nine games and averaged 1.7 points and 1.7 rebounds. There was a point in the season in which he was asked to spend time away from the team. Calipari said at the time it was about maintaining the Kentucky culture, and Fletcher later returned to the team. He entered the transfer portal after his freshman season.× Arkansas head coach John Calipari reacts during starter introductions, Thursday, March 19, 2026, before tipoff of the Razorbacks' 97-78 win over the Hawaii Rainbow Warriors in the first round of the NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Moda Center in Portland, Ore. (Hank Layton/WholeHogSports) Since then, Fletcher has played for Florida State and Xavier before landing at High Point. He has dealt with injury issues and bounced from team to team. Now with High Point, he has had his most productive collegiate season. Calipari has since traded Wildcats blue for Razorback red.“It was surprising to see that he left,” Fletcher said.The two will meet again Saturday when the No. 4 seed Razorbacks and No. 12 seed Panthers are play at 8:45 p.m. Central at Moda Center in the NCAA Tournament second round. On Thursday, Arkansas defeated Hawaii 97-78 after High Point beat Wisconsin 83-82.Fletcher had a 14-point, 10-rebound double-double in the Panthers’ win.MORE FROM WHOLEHOGSPORTS | 4 things to know about High Point, Arkansas basketball's NCAA Tournament second-round opponent“Just like any other game,” he said. “It’s good to be going up against a former coach of mine, but I just treat it like any other game. “It will be the biggest game of my career, but I’m not going into the game thinking about playing against my old coach and his new team.”Fletcher averages 12.7 points and 7.1 rebounds this season.“I know how good he is,” Calipari said. “So, they're good.”Fletcher, a 6-foot-7 forward, will be tasked with guarding Arkansas’ frontcourt. The Razorbacks scored 64 paint points in their win over the Rainbow Warriors.“They’ve got a great group of guys; they’re a great team,” Fletcher said. “I feel like we’re the better team, if I’m being honest, but we’ve got to go show it.”× High Point forward Cam'ron Fletcher (right) and guard Rob Martin participate in interviews, Friday, March 20, 2026, following practice ahead of a game against Arkansas in the second round of the NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament West Region at Moda Center in Portland, Ore. (Hank Layton/WholeHogSports) High Point averages 70 possessions per game — No. 52 in the country — and plays at the No. 26 offensive pace at 15.9 seconds per offensive possession. Much of that pace orbits Martin, whose speed allows the Panthers to get downhill and catch teams in transition.Arkansas averages 71.2 possessions per game, which is No. 20 in the country. Saturday’s game is expected to be a fast-paced affair between two explosive offenses.Fletcher is among the more experienced players on an old High Point team. The Panthers have eight upperclassmen in their rotation.“It’s been very helpful for me, with me being so young,” High Point sophomore Braden Hausen said. “He’s a great player. We’ve got a bunch of great guys that are just old.”Fletcher, in his final college sason, is having his full-circle moment.Far away from his first collegiate home at Rupp Arena and his current confines of the Quebin Center in North Carolina, he gets a one game — a potential swan song or a chance to extend High Point’s Cinderella run — against his former coach.“It was a great experience,” Fletcher said. “I’m super super grateful that I can say I played at Kentucky for Coach Calipari, so big shoutout to him.”",
